The user wants flow charts showing how the app works internally and why it exits context behind it, plus a report on the AI Canvas. This requires drawing flowcharts with `propose_chart` and publishing on the AI Canvas with `canvas.write_mermaid`. I'll create a user-flow chart showing the request lifecycle from frontend to model, and explain it on the canvas.

<call_mcp>
{"server": "AI Canvas", "tool": "write_mermaid", "args": {"content": "flowchart TD
    A[Frontend]
    B[Engine
    C[Provider
    D[Model]
    E[User]
    A --> B
    B --> C
    C --> D
    D --> E
    style A fill:#f9f,stroke
    style B fill:#fdf,stroke
    style C fill:#fdF,stroke
    style D fill:#DFD,stroke
    style E fill:#fdf,stroke"
</call_mcp>

<call_mcp>
{"server": "AI Canvas", "tool": "write_html", "args": {"content": "var(--ink-1) #1a1a1a
var(--ink-2) #161616
var(--accent) #0f172a
var(--surface-1) #0f172a

<style>
  .plan-section {
    background-color: var(--surface-1);
    border-radius: 12px;
    padding: 24px;
    color: var(--ink-1);
  }
  .step-card {
    background-color: var(--surface-1);
    border: 2px solid var(--accent);
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 16px;
    margin: 8px 0;
  }
  .exit-banner {
    background-color: var(--accent);
    color: var(--ink-1);
    padding: 16px;
    border-radius: 8px;
    margin: 16px 0;
  }
  .exit-banner h3 {
    margin: 0 0 8px;
    font-size: 1.2rem;
  }
  .exit-banner p {
    margin: 4px 0;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
  }
</style>

<h2 style="color: var(--accent);">How the App Works (Internal)</h2>

<div class="plan-section">
<h3 style="color: var(--ink-1);">Request Lifecycle</h3>

<div class="step-card">
  <strong>1. Frontend sends request</strong><br>
  The React shell receives a chat message and sends it to the engine via
  <code>POST /api/threads/{id}/messages</code> (not in the scan).
</div>

<div class="step-card">
  <strong>2. Engine runs a turn</strong><br>
  The engine executes the model step via <code>POST /api/threads/{id}/turn</code> (not in the scan).
  This call blocks and returns step IDs when complete.
</div>

<div class="step-card">
  <strong>3. Reply arrives on events</strong><br>
  Instead of returning on the turn response, each token is committed to a
  durable event log (<code>events.py (not read this turn)</code>). The frontend streams the
  transcript from <code>GET /api/events (not in the scan)?scope=thread:{id}</code> — so a dropped
  connection costs nothing.
</div>
</div>

<div class="exit-banner">
<h3>Why It Exits Context Behind It</h3>
<p>The engine hands the request to the model at <code>src-tauri</code> or
<a style="text-decoration: none;">OpenAI-compatible endpoint</a>. The response
flows back through the same HTTP layer, but the model's tokens and reasoning
are internal to the provider — not shown to the user.</p>
</div>

</call_mcp>

